<%# "Your account": the h1, then four cards in the settings card frame
    (`Admin::Ui::SettingsCardComponent`), 560px wide -- Profile and Notes in
    one form with one footer, Password, a form of its own with its own
    "Change password" footer and the promise about sessions as its card's
    intro, and Sessions, in no form (see it below). Every signed-in user changes their own password here, behind their
    current one, and the one profile field, their name. A deliberate submit,
    never autosave, like the Users module's own forms.

    Two forms, so two footers: each `Admin::Ui::SaveBarComponent` is the footer of
    its own form and counts only that form's changes (one Leave prompt serves
    both). The first form is the person's preferences -- the name and the two
    Notes switches (preferences save the same way; a password change is a
    separate, secure step). Neither Save may throw away what the other form
    holds:
    - "Save changes" keeps the screen (a Turbo Stream answer, see the controller),
      so the password fields stay as typed;
    - "Change password" re-renders the screen, so it waits for the first form:
      while the name or a Notes switch is unsaved it is locked, with "Save or
      discard your changes first." (`after:`: an instant answer that reloads
      the screen waits for the pending changes).
    The current password alone is nothing to save and is what a password
    manager fills in on load: it is not counted (`data-save-bar-ignore`), or
    every visit here would end in "Leave without saving?".
    The first form's footer stands under both its cards; the Password card's
    footer -- the footer of that one card -- sits INSIDE it, under a line
    (`contained: true`), and carries the "Forgot your current password?" link
    at the far end.
    Every string is a key: `admin.accounts` in `accounts.en.yml`, and the Notes
    card's, `admin.accounts.notes`, in `notes.en.yml` with notes' other wording.
    The sessions sentence is the service's own rule
    (`Users::OwnerProtection#end_sessions_after_password_change`: the acting
    session stays, the user's other sessions go). No typed password is ever
    rendered back: the inputs have no `value`. %>
<% content_for :title, t("admin.accounts.title") %>
<% field_errors = @field_errors || {} %>
<%# Whose answer this page is, when it is the password form's: "refused"
    (drawn again, 422), or after its redirect "saved" or "limited". %>
<% password_answer = @password_answer || flash[:password_answer] %>
<% password_errors = password_answer == "limited" ? [ t("admin.accounts.password.limited") ] : (field_errors.presence || Array(@errors)) %>
<div class="max-w-[560px]">
  <h1 class="mb-4 text-h1 text-ink"><%= t("admin.accounts.title") %></h1>

  <%# The page's summary is the Profile and Notes form's. The password form's
      answer, refused or saved, is said inside its own card, where the form
      is (see the card below). %>
  <%= render Admin::Ui::ErrorSummaryComponent.new(errors: Array(@profile_errors)) %>

  <%# What the form shows: the account as stored, or, after a refusal, what
      was sent (`@profile_values`, see the controller). %>
  <% profile = @profile_values || { name: Current.user.name, notes_sound: Current.user.notes_sound, notes_desktop: Current.user.notes_desktop } %>
  <%= form_with url: admin_account_profile_path(**admin_url_options), method: :patch, id: "profile-form", class: "mb-8",
        data: save_bar_form_data(dirty: Array(@profile_errors).any?) do %>
    <%= render Admin::Ui::SettingsCardComponent.new(heading_id: "account-profile", heading: t("admin.accounts.profile.title"), icon: :user, family: :people, class: "mb-4") do %>
      <div class="p-4">
        <%= label_tag "name", t("admin.accounts.profile.name"), class: setup_label_class %>
        <%= text_field_tag "name", profile[:name], id: "name", maxlength: 120, autocomplete: "name", class: setup_input_class %>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <%# Notes: two settings of the person's, saved with this
        form's footer like any configuration -- never autosaved, never
        through the write queue. A hidden "0" ahead of each box makes an
        unticked switch submit; `admin--saving--save-bar` does not count a hidden
        input, so the box alone is.

        The SETTING is the person's and follows them to every browser; the
        PERMISSION to notify is this browser's. The lines under the second
        switch are about the permission and are shown ONLY WHILE THAT SWITCH
        IS ON -- as it stands in the page, saved or not: turning it on shows
        the line at once (`admin--notes--permission`, which holds the switch
        as its target). Not asked yet: the accent-tinted line with
        "Allow in this browser", which asks at once, in the click, outside
        the save -- the button is `type="button"` with no name, so it is no
        field of the form and is never locked by it. The accent tint, not a
        notice: it is an offer to act, not a state. Allowed: nothing.
        Blocked: the warn notice, no button. Both lines are drawn hidden and
        the script shows the one that applies, inside one status region that
        speaks for them, so the notice writes no role of its own
        (`role: nil`). The permission is changed in the browser's own
        settings too, so it is read again whenever the window is looked at
        again. %>
    <%= render Admin::Ui::SettingsCardComponent.new(heading_id: "account-notes", heading: t("admin.accounts.notes.title"), icon: :message_square, family: :people,
          intro: t("admin.accounts.notes.description")) do %>
      <div class="border-b border-line px-4 py-3.5">
        <label class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4 text-sm text-ink">
          <span><%= t("admin.accounts.notes.notes_sound") %></span>
          <%= hidden_field_tag "notes_sound", "0", id: nil %>
          <%= check_box_tag "notes_sound", "1", profile[:notes_sound], role: "switch", class: "cs-toggle" %>
        </label>
      </div>
      <div class="px-4 py-3.5" data-controller="admin--notes--permission" data-state="unsupported"
           data-action="visibilitychange@document->admin--notes--permission#render focus@window->admin--notes--permission#render">
        <label class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4 text-sm text-ink">
          <span><%= t("admin.accounts.notes.notes_desktop") %></span>
          <%= hidden_field_tag "notes_desktop", "0", id: nil %>
          <%= check_box_tag "notes_desktop", "1", profile[:notes_desktop], role: "switch", class: "cs-toggle",
                data: { "admin--notes--permission-target": "switch", action: "change->admin--notes--permission#render" } %>
        </label>
        <div role="status">
          <div class="mt-2 flex items-center justify-between gap-3 rounded-card bg-accent-tint px-3 py-2.5 text-small text-ink" data-admin--notes--permission-target="ask" hidden>
            <span><%= t("admin.accounts.notes.permission.ask") %></span>
            <%= render Admin::Ui::ButtonComponent.new(label: t("admin.accounts.notes.permission.allow"), variant: :secondary, size: :small, type: "button",
                  class: "shrink-0", data: { action: "admin--notes--permission#allow" }) %>
          </div>
          <%= render Admin::Ui::NoticeComponent.new(tone: :warn, size: :compact, role: nil, class: "mt-2", hidden: true,
                data: { "admin--notes--permission-target": "blocked" }) do %><%= t("admin.accounts.notes.permission.blocked") %><% end %>
        </div>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <%= render Admin::Ui::SaveBarComponent.new(form_id: "profile-form", cancel_url: admin_root_path(**admin_url_options)) %>
  <% end %>

  <%= form_with url: admin_account_password_path(**admin_url_options), method: :patch, id: "password-form", data: save_bar_form_data(dirty: Array(@errors).any?, after: "profile-form") do %>
    <%# `#password` is the card's anchor: what a link to "change your
        password" names, and where the form's own answer comes back to.
        `scroll-mt`: clear of whatever is stuck to the top.
        Drawn as the password form's own answer, saved or refused:
        `admin--account--password-answer` puts `#password` in the address, the
        card in view and the focus on the message. %>
    <%= render Admin::Ui::SettingsCardComponent.new(heading_id: "account-password", heading: t("admin.accounts.password.title"), icon: :key_round, family: :neutral,
          intro: t("admin.accounts.password.sessions"), id: "password", class: "scroll-mt-6",
          data: (password_answer ? { controller: "admin--account--password-answer" } : {})) do %>
      <div class="px-4 pt-4">
        <%# The form's answer, at the top of the card's body: under the
            sentence about sessions, above the fields, where the eye is. A
            refusal is the error summary, whose lines link to their fields
            (each field keeps its own line too); the service's own sentences
            stand when it left no per-field errors (a denial, too many tries).
            A save is the compact ok notice, a status. Either takes the focus
            (`admin--account--password-answer`). %>
        <%= render Admin::Ui::ErrorSummaryComponent.new(errors: password_errors,
              field_ids: { current_password: "current_password", password: "new_password", password_confirmation: "password_confirmation" }) %>
        <% if password_answer == "saved" %>
          <%= render Admin::Ui::NoticeComponent.new(tone: :ok, size: :compact, class: "mb-4", tabindex: "-1", data: { password_saved: "" }) do %><%= t("admin.accounts.password.changed") %><% end %>
        <% end %>
        <div class="mb-4">
          <%= render Admin::Ui::PasswordFieldComponent.new(name: "current_password", id: "current_password", label: t("admin.accounts.password.current"),
                autocomplete: "current-password", autofocus: true, errors: field_errors[:current_password], "data-save-bar-ignore": true) %>
        </div>

        <div class="mb-4">
          <%# Its id is `new_password`: `password` is the card's, the page's
              anchor, and an id names one element. The field's name is `password`. %>
          <%= render Admin::Ui::PasswordFieldComponent.new(name: "password", id: "new_password", label: t("admin.accounts.password.new"), meter: true, errors: field_errors[:password]) %>
        </div>

        <div class="mb-4">
          <%= render Admin::Ui::PasswordFieldComponent.new(name: "password_confirmation", id: "password_confirmation", label: t("admin.accounts.password.confirmation"),
                errors: field_errors[:password_confirmation]) %>
        </div>
      </div>
      <%# The card's own footer, inside it: the card is short and its footer is where it ends. %>
      <%= render Admin::Ui::SaveBarComponent.new(form_id: "password-form", cancel_url: admin_root_path(**admin_url_options), save_label: t("admin.accounts.password.submit"), save_data: { password_submit: true }, contained: true) do %>
      <%# The reset flow is public and signs nobody out by being visited. It
          mails a link, so the link is offered only while mail works
          (`MailSettings#ready?`: Working or Last send failed), as on the
          sign-in page; the request page is closed otherwise. Opened in the
          page, not the frame: it is a different layout. %>
        <% if MailSettings.current.ready? %>
          <%= link_to t("admin.accounts.password.forgot"), new_admin_password_path, data: { turbo_frame: "_top" }, class: Admin::Ui::ButtonComponent.classes(:tertiary, size: :small) %>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>

  <%# Sessions: after both forms and in neither, so nothing in it is a
      change a save bar counts or "Leave without saving?" asks about. Its
      endings are forms of their own (`Admin::AccountSessionsController`);
      "Sign out everywhere else", which draws the screen again, waits for
      both forms (`Admin::AccountScreen::SessionsFootComponent`). The
      sessions are the controller's (`@sessions`). %>
  <%= render Admin::AccountScreen::SessionsCardComponent.new(sessions: @sessions, current: Current.session,
        url_options: admin_url_options, class: "mt-8") %>
</div>
